{"tool_name":"snpsnap","contents":["oeb","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"snpsnap","home":"http://www.broadinstitute.org/mpg/snpsnap/","summary":"A webserver that enables SNP-based enrichment analysis by providing matched sets of SNPs that can be used to calibrate background expectations.","addition_date":"2017-08-03T19:00:10Z","last_update_date":"2018-12-10T12:58:45Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:snpsnap","credit":[{"typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"https://data.broadinstitute.org/mpg/snpsnap/contact.html"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://data.broadinstitute.org/mpg/snpsnap/documentation.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Genome annotation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0362"},{"term":"Genome comparison","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3209"}]}],"name":"SNPsnap","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"mbs_import","publication":[{"metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 The Author 2014. Published by Oxford University Press. All rights reserved.An important computational step following genome-wide association studies (GWAS) is to assess whether disease or trait-associated single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) enrich for particular biological annotations. SNP-based enrichment analysis needs to account for biases such as co-localization of GWAS signals to gene-dense and high linkage disequilibrium (LD) regions, and correlations of gene size, location and function. The SNPsnap Web server enables SNP-based enrichment analysis by providing matched sets of SNPs that can be used to calibrate background expectations. Specifically, SNPsnap efficiently identifies sets of randomly drawn SNPs that are matched to a set of query SNPs based on allele frequency, number of SNPs in LD, distance to nearest gene and gene density.","authors":[{"name":"Hirschhorn J.N."},{"name":"Pers T.H."},{"name":"Timshel P."}],"citationCount